WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.320
Cloud GPU workloads can become expensive quickly because they combine high-value

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.340
accelerator capacity with fast-changing engineering demand.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:10.600
A single experiment, notebook, training job, evaluation run,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.480
or inference deployment may be reasonable by itself.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.375
The cost grows when many of those activities run without clear ownership, limits,

00:00:17.375 --> 00:00:18.080
or cleanup.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.200
Idle time is one of the most common sources of waste.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.800
A notebook can be open while no useful work is running.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.425
A worker can wait for data while the GPU is allocated.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.400
A training run can fail early but leave related resources behind.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.160
The bill reflects allocated resources, not only productive model progress.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.744
GPU cost is also more than accelerator time.

00:00:34.744 --> 00:00:39.280
Datasets, checkpoints, logs, artifacts, managed orchestration, monitoring,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.995
storage snapshots, and data movement all affect the final cost.

00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:46.590
Production inference adds another pattern: cost follows demand, latency targets,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:48.545
scaling rules, and model efficiency.

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:51.640
The practical lesson is that cost control is an engineering discipline.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.390
It should be designed into workflow defaults, permissions, schedules, job queues,

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.280
shutdown policies, monitoring, and review habits.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.715
Waiting for a surprise bill before adding controls usually means the system is already

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:03.040
hard to understand.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.080
Strong ownership matters.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.565
Teams should know who owns a workload, why it exists, what value it supports,

00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:08.990
and when it should stop.

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.600
When technical decisions are connected to financial impact,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.280
engineers can reduce waste without blocking useful work.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.105
The best cost conversations are specific: which job, which owner, which purpose,

00:01:18.105 --> 00:01:19.670
which limit, and which cleanup rule.
